-- title:
--   Norm-ℓ elements of an Eichler order meet its prime Hecke set
-- statement:
--   Let $q,q'$ be primes, $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$, and suppose $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ satisfies `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q q'`: $a>0$ or $b>0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ every nonzero element of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ is a unit precisely when $q\in v$ or $q'\in v$. Let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ that is a maximal order, i.e. an order (containing $1$, closed under multiplication, spanning the algebra over $\mathbb{Q}$, and finitely generated) with no strictly larger order above it. Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and $R$ a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule with `IsEichlerOrder R N`, i.e. $R=\Lambda_1\cap\Lambda_2$ for maximal orders $\Lambda_1,\Lambda_2$ with relative index $[\Lambda_1:R]=N$, and assume $R\le\Lambda$. Let $\ell$ be a prime and $x\in R$ with $\mathrm{nrd}(x)=x_{0}^{2}-a x_{1}^{2}-b x_{2}^{2}+ab\,x_{3}^{2}=\ell$. Then some unit $h$ of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ has underlying element $x\otimes 1$ and lies in `primeHeckeSet R ℓ`: writing $\widehat{R}$ for [`Submodule.finiteAdeleBox R`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L14), the additive subgroup generated by the $z\otimes\alpha$ with $z\in R$ and $\alpha$ a finite adele integral at every place, one has $h\in\widehat{R}$, $\ell\cdot h^{-1}\in\widehat{R}$, $h^{-1}\notin\widehat{R}$ and $\ell^{-1}\cdot h\notin\widehat{R}$.
--
--   This is the bridge from global integral quaternions of reduced norm $\ell$ to the adelic prime Hecke set attached to an Eichler order of level $N$, the adelic incarnation of the degree-$\ell$ Hecke correspondence on the associated Shimura curve. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd part of the development, in the comparison of Atkin–Lehner/Hecke data on period lattices with level Hecke sets and in the construction of period maps for uniformised Hecke curves.
